\section{Why the names are supplied rather than parsed}{

A header can need cleaning -- \code{" AGE "}, \verb{"\\"AGE\\""} -- and the cleaned name
is what the specification declares. An \code{arrow::Dataset} has no \verb{names<-}, so
the lazy path could never rename after the fact; and renaming through
\code{dplyr} returns an \code{arrow_dplyr_query}, which has no \verb{$files}, so
\code{\link[=dta_table_change_signal]{dta_table_change_signal()}} could not fingerprint it and \code{check()} would
revalidate the table on every run. Supplying the cleaned names at open time
and skipping the header line is the only form that works for both, so both
use it -- including for a header that needed no cleaning, so that there is
one code path rather than two.

The header is read once, by \code{\link[=dta_delim_header_names]{dta_delim_header_names()}}. Before this was
factored out, the eager reader read a file with a padded header TWICE: once
to learn the names, then again with the declared types pinned.
}

\section{Why every column is read as text}{

When \code{specs} is supplied, EVERY column is pinned to \code{utf8} -- not only the
declared ones. Three reasons, in order of how badly they bite:
\enumerate{
\item \strong{The two paths must agree.} A lazy dataset infers its schema from the
